  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered to numerous showers and storms this \nafternoon and early this evening in Northern Maine. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSome showers and storms (40-60 percent chance of showers, 15-30\npercent chance of storms) the rest of this afternoon into early\nevening over Northern Maine. Anticipate areas from Bangor \nsouth/east remaining dry due to too much of a marine influence. \nBest shot at a couple stronger storms is over the North Woods, \nwhere a couple storms could bring some gusty winds and small \nhail. So far activity has been quite unimpressive, but the\ndynamics improve some in the mid to late afternoon as an upper\nlevel shortwave approaches from the southwest. Activity will\nquickly dissipate after dark. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Areas of dense fog possible again tonight (Monday\nnight). Any fog would impact travel conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAreas of fog appear likely again tonight thanks to light winds \nand a decent amount of low-level moisture. There may be a \nslightly later onset to the fog in the evening this evening \ncompared to last night as we have a bit of breeze this afternoon\nthat still needs to come down, but still think the fog could be\nlocally dense by later tonight. The best chance of dense fog is\nnear the Downeast coast, though dense fog is possible anywhere.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chance of showers and thunderstorms from Bangor\nnorth Tuesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nMore showers and storms could occur Tuesday afternoon for\neverywhere except right near the coast, with a 30-50 percent\nchance. With colder air aloft and an upper trough moving \nthrough, some of the storms could have some small hail. Not \nexpecting anything severe though.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...Thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon and\nevening, some of which may be strong with gusty winds and small\nhail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front crosses the area Friday afternoon and evening on \nthe leading edge of a full latitude trough pushing into the \nregion, with the added support of a northern stream shortwave \npushing through the trough around that time. With the potential \nfor 500-1500 J/kg of instability and 25-35KT of bulk shear, \nthere is a risk of some locally strong thunderstorms ahead of \nthe front, with the main threat expected to be strong gusty \nwinds. The greatest chance for storms is likely to be \nconcentrated over northern and central areas while more southern\nareas are likely to remain too stable due to southerly flow.",
